# Committed fixtures are records, and git will edit them.
#
# A connector's offline fixture records what a source said when it was asked. Git
# normalizes line endings on checkout, and for most fixtures nobody notices. For
# some, the line endings ARE the property under test — a bulk export served with
# classic-Mac CR endings, a register that serves CRLF — and normalizing them
# silently deletes the thing the fixture exists to pin. The test then passes
# against what git produced rather than against what the source served, which is
# the failure a hermetic fixture was supposed to prevent.
#
# A derived repository found this the way you would expect to: by committing a
# fixture, watching the test pass, and discovering later that "the committed
# record was of what git did rather than what the register said."
#
# Mark those paths `-text` as you add each connector, and say in the comment
# which property is being protected. A bare `-text` with no comment gets deleted
# by the next person tidying this file.
#
# Example:
#
#   # The SoS bulk exports use classic-Mac CR line endings and these fixtures
#   # exist to pin that. Normalizing them would delete the property under test.
#   crates/committee-graph/data/*.csv -text
#
#   # The lobbying register serves CRLF. Same reasoning.
#   crates/ohio-lobbying/data/*.html -text
